WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.819 --> 00:00:06.840
The one that was really dangerous for me was
the time that we needed to distribute the

00:00:06.840 --> 00:00:11.166
books and notebooks to the very far village.

00:00:11.166 --> 00:00:16.449
I was working with an international organization
teaching the girls out there.

00:00:16.449 --> 00:00:19.353
So we put all the books in the 
back of the car.

00:00:19.353 --> 00:00:23.439
Of course, my father-in-law and a driver 
accompany me.

00:00:23.439 --> 00:00:27.514
During the way, crossing the road, we're stopped
by the Taliban.

00:00:27.514 --> 00:00:31.812
They keep everyone separated so they cannot
listen to each other.

00:00:31.812 --> 00:00:35.132
So, I was the only woman sitting out there.

00:00:35.132 --> 00:00:40.070
All the trunks and backs of our cars 
were full of the books.

00:00:40.070 --> 00:00:45.242
I was really sure that this time they will
definitely kill me.

00:00:45.242 --> 00:00:47.477
This was in 1999.

00:00:47.477 --> 00:00:50.414
When the Taliban first ruled Afghanistan.

00:00:50.414 --> 00:00:53.549
They oppressed Afghans with laws 
drawn from tribal codes

00:00:53.549 --> 00:00:56.481
and a strict interpretation of Islam.

00:00:56.481 --> 00:01:00.530
Their control was total and brutal.

00:01:00.530 --> 00:01:03.380
Two decades later, the Taliban 
have taken back power.

00:01:03.380 --> 00:01:07.430
Every year, I've seen how the Taliban, they
grew stronger.

00:01:07.430 --> 00:01:10.373
They have taken over Afghanistan twice.

00:01:10.373 --> 00:01:12.438
Not because they're good at governing.

00:01:12.438 --> 00:01:17.280
But because of the other actors in Afghanistan,
because of their failures.

00:01:17.280 --> 00:01:20.347
"Afghanistan falls to Taliban control."

00:01:32.783 --> 00:01:34.085
"Afghanistan.

00:01:34.085 --> 00:01:37.921
Soviet troops were everywhere throughout the
capital and the country."

00:01:37.921 --> 00:01:41.110
"Moscow claimed they were there because they'd
been invited."

00:01:41.110 --> 00:01:44.055
"That version of events found few believers
elsewhere."

00:01:44.055 --> 00:01:48.464
In 1979, the Soviet Union invaded 
Afghanistan to back an ally

00:01:48.464 --> 00:01:50.787
the new Communist Afghan government.

00:01:50.787 --> 00:01:54.653
Together, they wanted to transform the country
into a socialist state.

00:01:54.653 --> 00:01:55.847
But they faced resistance.

00:01:55.847 --> 00:02:01.390
"The Afghan crisis won't be easily dismissed,
and a glance at the map shows why."

00:02:01.390 --> 00:02:03.659
Afghanistan is a deeply divided country.

00:02:03.659 --> 00:02:07.936
At the time, only a small fraction of the population
lived in a few major cities

00:02:07.936 --> 00:02:10.414
including some who supported the government.

00:02:10.414 --> 00:02:15.636
But the majority of Afghans lived in rural
areas where people were generally poor, conservative,

00:02:15.636 --> 00:02:19.290
and very devoted to Islam and tribal traditions.

00:02:19.290 --> 00:02:23.408
For many, authority came from tribal leaders
of their respective ethic groups.

00:02:23.408 --> 00:02:25.307
Not the government in Kabul.

00:02:25.307 --> 00:02:28.780
So when the Communist Afghan government 
tried to enforce land reforms 

00:02:28.780 --> 00:02:32.620
and abolish social codes, 
rural Afghans revolted.

00:02:32.620 --> 00:02:36.776
They formed independent militias, but were
collectively known as the mujahideen.

00:02:36.776 --> 00:02:38.749
"These are real mujahideen."

00:02:38.749 --> 00:02:41.485
"They are scattered and almost 
impossible to unite."

00:02:41.485 --> 00:02:45.821
"They operate best in small groups and in
their home the mountains."

00:02:45.821 --> 00:02:49.980
The Soviet Army managed to occupy the cities,
but met fierce resistance from the mujahideen

00:02:49.980 --> 00:02:51.670
in the rural areas.

00:02:51.670 --> 00:02:56.920
The Soviets responded by pounding the countryside
with bombs, and razing entire villages.

00:02:56.920 --> 00:03:00.530
But it galvanized more people 
to join the rebellion.

00:03:00.530 --> 00:03:04.277
Thousands of muslim volunteers from around
the world joined the mujahideen.

00:03:04.277 --> 00:03:09.121
Countries eager to gain influence in Afghanistan
started arming and funding militias.

00:03:09.121 --> 00:03:13.620
Even the US sent weapons to drain the Soviets,
their main rival.

00:03:13.620 --> 00:03:17.370
The support allowed the mujahideen 
to fight for 10 years.

00:03:17.370 --> 00:03:21.512
Around a million Afghans died and 
6 million were displaced.

00:03:24.578 --> 00:03:27.100
In 1989, the Soviets gave up and left.

00:03:27.100 --> 00:03:30.727
3 years after that, the Afghan Communist 
government fell.

00:03:30.727 --> 00:03:32.600
But the violence didn’t end.

00:03:32.600 --> 00:03:34.650
The mujahideen groups turned on each other.

00:03:34.650 --> 00:03:39.038
And by 1992, Afghanistan was consumed 
by civil war.

00:03:39.038 --> 00:03:43.104
Door to door, street to street, road to road.

00:03:43.104 --> 00:03:46.190
Region to region, province to province.

00:03:46.190 --> 00:03:47.940
There was always fighting.

00:03:47.940 --> 00:03:49.330
This is Sweeta Noori.

00:03:49.330 --> 00:03:51.445
She was born and raised in Afghanistan.

00:03:51.445 --> 00:03:55.161
And has worked there for years promoting 
education and women’s rights.

00:03:55.161 --> 00:04:00.874
They start searching the houses to find if
there is a beautiful or young woman

00:04:00.874 --> 00:04:03.021
so they can marry by force.

00:04:03.021 --> 00:04:07.787
It was really the worst time.

00:04:07.787 --> 00:04:10.200
Kandahar was especially violent.

00:04:10.200 --> 00:04:13.840
Several mujahideen and criminal groups terrorized
the population.

00:04:13.840 --> 00:04:17.410
In the spring of 1994, a group of locals here
had enough.

00:04:17.410 --> 00:04:21.175
They asked this man, Mohammed Omar, for help.

00:04:21.175 --> 00:04:25.540
He had fought in the Soviet war, before becoming
a teacher, or mullah, at an Islamic school

00:04:25.540 --> 00:04:27.200
called a madrassa.

00:04:28.058 --> 00:04:32.430
He and a few other mullahs gathered some students,
and drove out the mujahideen.

00:04:32.430 --> 00:04:35.337
When more students joined, they captured the
whole district.

00:04:35.337 --> 00:04:37.501
Then Kandahar City.

00:04:37.501 --> 00:04:42.570
This group became known as the Taliban, which
means ‘the students’, in Pashto.

00:04:42.570 --> 00:04:47.626
As the sole militia in Kandahar, they brought
peace for the first time in years.

00:04:47.626 --> 00:04:50.173
And that’s exactly why many people 
supported them.

00:04:50.173 --> 00:04:56.060
If the mullahs and imams say that the color
of the milk is black, all the people accepting

00:04:56.060 --> 00:04:58.250
that because it comes from mullahs.

00:04:58.250 --> 00:05:03.490
Why not giving them an opportunity 
to take over the government.

00:05:03.490 --> 00:05:07.094
At this point, the Taliban were all members
of Afghanistan’s largest ethnic group

00:05:07.094 --> 00:05:08.594
the Pashtuns.

00:05:08.594 --> 00:05:12.928
Which made it easier for them to take over
the mostly Pashtun-areas in the south.

00:05:12.928 --> 00:05:18.000
Then they moved west and north, defeating
some warlords and bribing others to join them.

00:05:18.000 --> 00:05:21.889
As they gained territory, they also found
a way to fund their expansion.

00:05:21.889 --> 00:05:25.098
By taking over these highways they raised
millions through taxes.

00:05:25.098 --> 00:05:28.025
And by gaining control of Afghanistan's 
poppy-growing regions

00:05:28.025 --> 00:05:30.551
they profited from the illegal opium trade.

00:05:30.551 --> 00:05:33.830
But the most crucial support came from a neighbor.

00:05:33.830 --> 00:05:37.139
Pakistan was worried that one of these mujahideen
groups would take power

00:05:37.139 --> 00:05:39.200
and ally with its enemy, India.

00:05:39.200 --> 00:05:40.991
Effectively surrounding them.

00:05:40.991 --> 00:05:44.043
So they gave the Taliban tons of weapons
early on.

00:05:44.043 --> 00:05:49.541
In September 1996, the Taliban entered Kabul
and took control of Afghanistan.

00:05:54.280 --> 00:05:55.460
My name is Nagieb Khaja.

00:05:55.460 --> 00:05:58.404
I've been covering the war in Afghanistan
for 17 years.

00:05:58.404 --> 00:06:05.003
When the Taliban took Kabul in 1996, they
set up a government that was basically

00:06:05.003 --> 00:06:06.689
only their members.

00:06:06.689 --> 00:06:11.104
Both its leadership and fighting base were
predominantly Pashtun teachers and students.

00:06:11.104 --> 00:06:17.800
It was basically a very inexperienced government
without any practical knowledge

00:06:17.800 --> 00:06:19.753
about how to govern.

00:06:19.753 --> 00:06:24.167
Yet, they decided to remake the country based
on a religious ideology that took shape

00:06:24.167 --> 00:06:25.350
in their madrassas.

00:06:25.350 --> 00:06:31.621
What makes the Taliban special is that these
madrassas called Deobandi madrassas

00:06:31.621 --> 00:06:37.657
are inspired by a certain interpretation of Islam 
influenced by Pashtun culture.

00:06:37.657 --> 00:06:40.860
Music, television, and even flying kites 
were banned.

00:06:40.860 --> 00:06:42.808
Men had to grow beards.

00:06:42.808 --> 00:06:45.473
But the most draconian rules 
were placed on women.

00:06:45.473 --> 00:06:49.750
They weren't allowed to go out 
without a male relative.

00:06:49.750 --> 00:06:52.000
They couldn't educate themselves.

00:06:52.000 --> 00:06:54.530
They were practically banned from working.

00:06:54.530 --> 00:06:56.581
Many schools for girls went underground.

00:06:56.581 --> 00:06:58.593
Like the one Sweeta ran in Kabul.

00:06:58.593 --> 00:07:04.090
It was like only 20 meters away from 
the Taliban camp.

00:07:04.090 --> 00:07:09.020
I had like a good number of the students coming
to my school.

00:07:09.020 --> 00:07:11.400
It was all done in secret.

00:07:11.400 --> 00:07:15.586
And one day, the Taliban enter.

00:07:17.880 --> 00:07:22.030
So I stopped them in front of the door and
I asked them to wait.

00:07:22.030 --> 00:07:27.269
My students just notice that they hide the
books and take out their holy Qurans.

00:07:33.209 --> 00:07:36.450
They would start beating me.

00:07:36.450 --> 00:07:40.350
And same thing they would do with 
the family of the girls.

00:07:40.350 --> 00:07:43.489
Beatings, stonings, and public executions
were common punishments

00:07:43.489 --> 00:07:45.567
under the Taliban regime.

00:07:45.567 --> 00:07:49.219
It’s why most countries, except Pakistan,
Saudi Arabia and the UAE

00:07:49.219 --> 00:07:52.310
refused to recognize them 
as a legitimate government.

00:07:52.310 --> 00:07:54.867
But the Taliban soon found other allies.

00:07:54.867 --> 00:07:58.481
"The most wanted terrorist in the world: 
Osama bin Laden."

00:07:58.481 --> 00:08:00.680
"...by Afghanistan's ruling Taliban."

00:08:00.680 --> 00:08:05.210
"The ruling Taliban's refusal to surrender
suspected terrorist bin Laden."

00:08:05.210 --> 00:08:09.870
"The US will hold Afghanistan responsible
for any attack linked to bin Laden."

00:08:09.870 --> 00:08:13.100
"You're looking at, obviously a very disturbing
live shot there."

00:08:13.100 --> 00:08:15.080
"That is the World Trade Center..."

00:08:15.080 --> 00:08:19.730
After the 9/11 attacks, a US-led coalition
invaded Afghanistan to hunt down bin Laden

00:08:19.730 --> 00:08:21.410
and topple the Taliban.

00:08:22.053 --> 00:08:26.273
With the help of these mujahideen leaders
it took less than 3 months to capture Kabul.

00:08:26.273 --> 00:08:30.620
Bin Laden escaped but the Taliban regime surrendered
and asked for amnesty.

00:08:30.620 --> 00:08:31.990
The US refused.

00:08:31.990 --> 00:08:34.561
So basically, the Taliban, they don't get a choice. 

00:08:34.561 --> 00:08:39.750
They can either become prisoners 
or continue fighting.

00:08:39.750 --> 00:08:45.196
The Taliban leadership fled to Pakistan, while
most fighters went into hiding in rural Afghanistan.

00:08:45.196 --> 00:08:50.240
Then, the US promised to rebuild the country
and turn it into a democracy.

00:08:51.291 --> 00:08:54.854
It worked with the UN to set up an 
Afghan government and Army.

00:08:54.854 --> 00:08:59.130
And invited mujahideen leaders to run 
the local governments.

00:08:59.130 --> 00:09:02.630
Then they gave the government billions of
dollars to build roads, bridges, hospitals,

00:09:02.630 --> 00:09:05.580
and utilities in the cities and rural areas.

00:09:06.266 --> 00:09:09.170
But many of those projects never took shape.

00:09:09.170 --> 00:09:12.457
The Afghan government was deeply corrupt 
and stole millions.

00:09:12.457 --> 00:09:17.207
And in 2003, the US invaded Iraq and diverted
a lot of its money.

00:09:17.207 --> 00:09:19.639
What remained was spent mostly in the cities.

00:09:19.639 --> 00:09:21.953
While rural Afghanistan was neglected.

00:09:21.953 --> 00:09:24.250
And increasingly under attack.

00:09:26.801 --> 00:09:31.830
While the US was trying to rebuild Afghanistan,
it was simultaneously waging a war on it.

00:09:31.830 --> 00:09:35.248
The American military was hunting down 
Taliban and al-Qaeda members

00:09:35.248 --> 00:09:37.551
largely in these rural areas.

00:09:37.551 --> 00:09:41.540
And funding and arming these mujahideen leaders
in exchange for their help.

00:09:41.540 --> 00:09:43.060
But it all backfired.

00:09:43.060 --> 00:09:46.519
Drone-strikes and night raids increasingly
killed innocent civilians.

00:09:46.519 --> 00:09:50.950
And the mujahideen leaders started terrorizing
people, effectively becoming warlords.

00:09:51.593 --> 00:09:56.652
"The suffering of Afghanistan in certain parts
of the country have not ended."

00:09:57.746 --> 00:10:00.450
"We still keep losing our civilian lives."

00:10:02.101 --> 00:10:04.624
"It's becoming to be heavy for us.

00:10:04.624 --> 00:10:06.350
It's not understandable anymore."

00:10:09.309 --> 00:10:14.800
Basically, the Americans, they created 
their own monsters.

00:10:14.800 --> 00:10:20.413
As early as 2004, the Taliban leadership regrouped
in Pakistan and started recruiting fighters.

00:10:20.413 --> 00:10:24.040
Like the 90s, they attracted Pashtun fighters
in the south and east.

00:10:24.040 --> 00:10:29.040
Butt this time they also attracted non-Pashtuns,
including Afghans from the west and north.

00:10:29.040 --> 00:10:33.260
But the thing that they have in common is
that they are from rural Afghanistan.

00:10:33.260 --> 00:10:36.050
They had been harassed by the local authorities.

00:10:36.050 --> 00:10:40.330
They had relatives being killed by 
US aerial bombings.

00:10:40.330 --> 00:10:43.391
They were caught between 
a rock and a hard place

00:10:43.391 --> 00:10:46.553
and they ended up choosing the hard place.

00:10:46.553 --> 00:10:48.937
And that was the Taliban.

00:10:48.937 --> 00:10:52.100
Soon they were ambushing 
US, NATO, and Afghan troops.

00:10:52.100 --> 00:10:55.680
And introducing more sophisticated tactics
like roadside bombs.

00:10:55.680 --> 00:10:57.340
And suicide attacks.

00:10:57.812 --> 00:11:01.870
Enlisting diverse groups, even those that
didn’t share their strict religious beliefs,

00:11:01.870 --> 00:11:06.680
made it possible for the Taliban to take territory
in many parts of the country.

00:11:06.680 --> 00:11:11.065
For funding, they relied on familiar tactics,
taxing highways and trading poppy.

00:11:11.065 --> 00:11:15.461
Plus, they continued to have help from Pakistan,
who not only protected Taliban leaders

00:11:15.461 --> 00:11:19.529
but also armed, funded, and trained their fighters.

00:11:19.529 --> 00:11:23.273
By 2008, the Taliban controlled huge swaths
of rural Afghanistan

00:11:23.273 --> 00:11:25.505
and even threatened some cities.

00:11:28.400 --> 00:11:33.322
"And as Commander in Chief, I have determined
that it is in our vital, national interest

00:11:33.322 --> 00:11:37.673
to send an additional 30,000 troops to Afghanistan."

00:11:37.673 --> 00:11:42.471
In 2009, the US responded by sending a surge
of troops for 18 months.

00:11:42.471 --> 00:11:46.672
They cleared major cities, but couldn’t
dislodge the Taliban from the rural areas.

00:11:48.151 --> 00:11:52.371
In 2012, the US reduced its troops and relied
on the Afghan Government and Army

00:11:52.371 --> 00:11:54.000
to lead the fight.

00:11:54.000 --> 00:11:58.230
But years of corruption had led many Afghans
to distrust the government.

00:11:58.230 --> 00:12:02.330
Leaving room for the Taliban to start governing
their own territories.

00:12:02.330 --> 00:12:07.060
But this version of the Taliban's governance
was more flexible than their rule in the 90s.

00:12:07.060 --> 00:12:13.190
In some places, their governance looked very
much like the old Taliban in the mid 90s.

00:12:13.190 --> 00:12:15.610
In other areas, they are much more pragmatic.

00:12:15.610 --> 00:12:21.970
They had girls' schools and they didn't practices,
you know, rules about beards and TV shows.

00:12:21.970 --> 00:12:26.578
It was proof that you had 
a very fragmented Taliban.

00:12:26.578 --> 00:12:32.455
That was key that allowed them to consolidate
power and wait for the US to leave.

00:12:36.229 --> 00:12:40.993
In February 2020, the Trump administration
went straight to the Taliban to strike a deal.

00:12:40.993 --> 00:12:43.203
They agreed to leave Afghanistan.

00:12:43.203 --> 00:12:47.310
In August 2021, the Biden administration 
delivered on that deal.

00:12:47.310 --> 00:12:50.574
As the final troops pulled out, 
the Taliban attacked.

00:12:50.574 --> 00:12:54.561
The world watched as the Afghan army surrendered,
the government fled

00:12:54.561 --> 00:12:57.709
and the Taliban entered Kabul unopposed.

00:12:59.232 --> 00:13:02.320
In September, the Taliban announced a new
interim government.

00:13:02.320 --> 00:13:03.623
And it looks familiar.

00:13:03.623 --> 00:13:05.527
Its top leadership is mostly Pashtun.

00:13:05.527 --> 00:13:08.578
Many of whom served in the 1990s regime.

00:13:08.578 --> 00:13:12.393
But their base, like their fighters, is far
more diverse.

00:13:12.393 --> 00:13:15.221
Which makes this new regime unpredictable.

00:13:15.221 --> 00:13:21.610
It will be a problem for the Taliban if they
don't tolerate variation of interpretation

00:13:21.610 --> 00:13:26.373
of Islam in the movement which could end up
with a violent conflict

00:13:26.373 --> 00:13:28.190
between the different parties.

00:13:28.190 --> 00:13:33.893
I'm convinced that they haven't agreed on
what kind of Afghanistan they want right now.

00:13:34.922 --> 00:13:41.490
There is a small window of hope if the international
community put force on the Taliban 

00:13:41.490 --> 00:13:46.680
just make sure that the women and people 
have their basic needs.

00:13:46.680 --> 00:13:49.493
Access to education, access to jobs.

00:13:49.493 --> 00:13:51.469
Access to freedom.

